==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|En-au-take on.ogg|a=AU}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|<,,took,taken>}}

# To [[acquire]], bring in, or [[introduce]].
#: {{ux|en|The ship '''took on''' cargo in Norfolk yesterday.}}
## To obtain the services of (a person) in exchange for remuneration; to give someone a job.
##: {{ux|en|'''Taking on''' a first employee should be done with care and consideration.}}
# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} To begin to [[have]] or [[exhibit]].
#: {{ux|en|In the dark, the teddy bear '''took on''' the appearance of a fearsome monster.}}
# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} To [[assume]] or [[take responsibility]] for.
#: {{ux|en|I'll '''take on''' the project if no one else will.}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''took on''' the children's welfare as an extra charge.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1987|magazine=Nigeria Magazine|volume=55-56|publisher=Government of Nigeria|page=27
|passage=This type of marriage was always fraught with divorce because in most cases the old creditor, instead of allowing one of his male children to marry the girl, would '''take''' her '''on''' himself. For the fact that flirting was a serious taboo within the society, the girl would bolt away{{...}}.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|author=Elaine McKewon|chapter=The Scarlet Mile|title=A Social History of Prostitution in Kalgoorlie, 1894-2004|publisher=University of Western Australia Press|page=74
|passage=She was 78, I think, and if there was only one customer, she’d '''take''' him '''on''' sometimes.}}
## {{lb|en|UK}} To accept (feedback).
##: {{ux|en|Thanks for your comments, I'll '''take''' them '''on'''.}}
# {{senseid|en|attempt to fight}} {{lb|en|idiomatic}} To attempt to [[fight]], [[compete]] with, or engage with.
#: {{ux|en|I don't recommend '''taking on''' that [[bully]], since he's bigger than you are.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1990|author=Robert H. Rimmer|title=The Harrad Experiment|publisher=Prometheus Books|page=93
|passage=“<!--...-->I’ll bet, despite the fact that the Tenhausens picked you as a soul-mate for June Atterman, that if Beth Hillyer took of[f] her clothes and shook herself at you, you’d point right in the air and be ready to '''take''' her '''on'''.”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|author=George Gmelch|title=Behind the smile: The working lives of Caribbean tourism|page=129|publisher=Indiana University Press
|passage=I don't find that sexy. I tell her to take her time and try to be friends and work her way in. Then I might see something nice in her and '''take''' her '''on'''.}}
# {{lb|en|football}} To (attempt to) [[dribble]] round (an opposition player).
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=22 May 2016|author=Phil McNulty| title=Crystal Palace 1-2 Manchester United| work=BBC|url=http://www.bbc.com/sport/football/36292265| passage=He drifted past four Palace players and '''took on''' two more before crossing to the far post, where {{w|Marouane Fellaini|Fellaini}} touched on for Mata to score. It was a momentum-shifting moment.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260905034215/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/36292265|archivedate=5 September 2026}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|colloquial}} To [[catch on]], do well; to become popular.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1974|author=w:GB Edwards|title=The Book of Ebenezer Le Page|page=225|year_published=2007|location=New York
|passage=He had enough money to stock it well, and it '''took on'''; but the side of the business he did best on was his travelling shop.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|idiomatic}} To [[grieve]] or be [[concerned]] (''about'' something or someone).
#* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|[[s:Moby-Dick (1851) US edition/Chapter 16|chapter 16]]|text=But I am one of those that never '''take on''' about princely fortunes, and am quite content if the world is ready to board and lodge me, while I am putting up at this grim sign of the Thunder Cloud.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1955|author=w:Patrick White|title=w:The Tree of Man|url=https://archive.org/details/treeofmannovel00whit/page/198/mode/1up?q=taking+on|chapter=13|pages=198–199|publisher=Viking|location=New York
|passage=So she hung crying, lopsided and ludicrous on the seat of the buggy {{...}}. People passing looked at her and wondered why she was '''taking on'''. There was something almost obscene about a strong, healthy woman blubbering in the sunlight in that public place.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|UK|military}} To [[enlist]] into [[military]] [[service]].

